<urn:atm:triage:finding/AX4-QA2-003>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AX4-QA2-003" ;
  triage:title "New sealed AsyncTaskLedgerReader trait has no dedicated boundary record; the allowed_test_double_paths entry on BOUNDARY-TaskStore gives it no mechanical lint enforcement" ;
  triage:description "AX.4 QA-2 (quality-mgr ax4-qa-r2, PR #1194 Final Quality Report 2026-09-05T10:24Z; found by ruthless-boundary-qa, confirmed by quality-mgr) at head 193821c92. The only boundary change this round adds crate::testing::InMemoryTaskLedgerReader to [testing].allowed_test_double_paths in boundaries/atm-storage/task-store.toml, whose [public].trait is TaskStore. .just/lint_boundaries.py keys its implementation-violation scanner off record.public_trait (_trait_impl_pattern, ~lines 2453-2456 and 2522-2532), so it only searches for impl TaskStore for X and never impl AsyncTaskLedgerReader for X. The new sealed trait (crates/atm-storage/src/contract.rs, implemented in crates/atm-storage-rusqlite/src/task_ledger_reader.rs) therefore has no boundary record and no mechanical enforcement, unlike its sibling AsyncMailboxReader which has the paired records boundaries/atm-storage/async-mailbox-reader.toml and boundaries/atm-storage-rusqlite/async-mailbox-reader-sqlite.toml. sealed::Sealed still prevents external impls at compile time, so this is an enforcement-consistency gap, not a live leak. Fix (ruled by fenix): add boundaries/atm-storage/async-task-ledger-reader.toml (owner atm-storage, public.trait AsyncTaskLedgerReader) and boundaries/atm-storage-rusqlite/async-task-ledger-reader-sqlite.toml (owner atm-storage-rusqlite) mirroring the AsyncMailboxReader pair, move the InMemoryTaskLedgerReader allowlist entry to the new record and remove it from task-store.toml." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-ax" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "RBQA" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
